        "thinking": "I'm working through the verb तुजिँ, noting it's an इदित् root taking नुम् insertion (तुञ्ज्), and recalling there are actually two homophonous roots — one भ्वादि meaning \"to protect\" and one चुरादि meaning \"to injure/strengthen\" — so I need to figure out which sense and conjugation class applies here.\n\nSince नुम् roots like वञ्च्, स्रंस्, पत् take special नीक् augment in certain derivations but तुञ्ज् doesn't fall under that rule, I'm reasoning through the यङ् (intensive) formation: reduplicating to get तो + तुञ्ज्, and since the root is इदित् (not अनिदित्), the नलोप rule doesn't apply, giving तोतुञ्ज्य् as the यङन्त base. Then I'm working out what happens when णिच् (causative) is added to this यङन्त form, applying the rule about य-लोप after हल् consonants.\n\nApplying यस्य हलः, I remove the य् after the ञ्ज् cluster since णिच् is आर्धधातुक, leaving तोतुञ्ज् + इ. Checking whether गुण applies to the उपधा — since the penultimate letter is न् rather than a लघु vowel, गुण doesn't trigger, so the form settles as तोतुञ्जि.\n\nNow moving to लुङ् कर्तरि परस्मैपद: the णिजन्त form goes through चङ् by णिश्रिद्रुस्रुभ्यः कर्तरि चङ्, with प्र उपसर्ग and अट् augment preceding the reduplicated चङ् form of तोतुञ्जि. In चङ्, णि लोप applies since it's अनिट्, giving तोतुञ्ज् + अ, and I'm checking whether णौ चङ्युपधाया ह्रस्वः applies — but since the उपधा here is न् rather than a vowel, that rule doesn't seem to come into play either.\n\nI'm checking whether the उपधा vowel-shortening rule applies at all, since the उपधा in तोतुञ्जि is ञ् rather than a vowel — so it seems ह्रस्व doesn't apply. Then considering चङि's reduplication rule, since the धातु already underwent reduplication from यङ्, I wonder if there's a principle preventing a second द्वित्व, and I'm trying to recall parallel examples like यङन्त+णिच्+चङ् forms such as \"बोभूयि\" → \"अबोभवत्\" to see how this pattern typically resolves.\n\nI'm checking what \"अनभ्यासस्य\" actually qualifies — it seems to mean the dhatu itself shouldn't already contain an abhyāsa, as explained in the Kāśikā gloss. I recall examples like जुहुवतुः being cited to test this restriction, and also forms like अचिकीर्षत् involving सन्नन्त + णिच् + चङ् combinations to see how the rule applies there.\n\nI'm trying to recall whether अनभ्यासस्य blocks reduplication from happening twice — the dhātu already carrying an abhyāsa (like in यङन्त forms) doesn't get a fresh abhyāsa in लिट्. Thinking through an example: भू → यङ् बोभूय → णिच् बोभूयि → लुङ् अबोभूयत्, where no second द्वित्व applies since the abhyāसा already exists from the यङ् formation.\n\nI'm trying to recall the exact condition for \"अनभ्यासस्य\" in 6.1.8 - whether reduplication is skipped when the dhatu already contains an abhyasa, like in cases such as जागृ (जजागार) or सन्नन्त forms. I want to ground this in actual grammatical examples rather than guessing at Vidyut's implementation details.\n\nNow I'm recalling the यङ्लुक् example: भू becomes बोभू, and in लिट् it forms बोभूव rather than undergoing a second reduplication, since \"अनभ्यासस्य\" blocks द्वित्व on a dhatu that already has an abhyasa from the यङ्लुक्.\n\nApplying this to चङ् forms, I work through तोतुञ्ज् + चङ् → अतोतुञ्जत्, and after prefixing प्र and applying सवर्णदीर्घ, I get प्रातोतुञ्जत् — checking this against the rule for ह्रस्व in चङ् before उपधा.\n\nI'm working through whether the य-लोप counts as स्थानिवत् for purposes of लघूपध गुण — checking if बेभिद् + इ would then take गुण to give बेभेदि, and examining how the Kashika frames the deletion of य after हल् in this rule.\n\nFollowing the Siddhanta Kaumudi's gloss, I see the real process: first only the initial य् is deleted (आदेः परस्य), then separately अतो लोपः deletes the following अ, and it's this second अ-लोप that is स्थानिवत् and blocks लघूपधगुण — giving the final form बेभिदिता rather than बेभेदिता.\n\nTurning to णिच् forms, I note बेभिदि similarly shows no गुण, though that's moot here since the उपधा is ञ्, not relevant to the vowel rule.\n\nNow checking the लुङ् derivation for प्र + अट् + तोतुञ्जि + चङ् + त्: णि is removed by णेरनिटि, leaving तोतुञ्ज् + अ + त्. I'm examining whether द्वित्व (reduplication) is blocked here by the अनभ्यास restriction, since तोतुञ्जि itself already contains an अभ्यास \"तो\" as its first syllable — which makes me think reduplication should indeed be blocked, though I want to double check whether the अनभ्यास condition is really about the धातु itself lacking an अभ्यास.\n\nI'm recalling the relevant sutra: लिटि धातोरनभ्यासस्य, meaning in लिट् (and by extension चङ् and सन्यङोः) the dhātu without an abhyāsa gets its first ekac doubled. This explains why सन् after यङ् doesn't reduplicate again — बोभूयिषति is the standard form, confirming consistency with this rule. So tentatively my answer for the question is प्रातोतुञ्जत्, though I still need to verify whether णत्व applies here, since there's no न in sight to trigger it.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the न् in प्रातोतुञ्जत् undergoes णत्व — actually it first becomes ञ् through the rule converting नुम् to anusvāra and then to परसवर्ण before झल्. Since प्र is a separate pada from the dhātu, and the नुम्-न् isn't part of the original ण-upadeśa dhātu, णत्व doesn't seem to apply here.\n\nMoving to the second question, I'm working through घृ (जुहोत्यादि, meaning क्षरणदीप्त्योः) with उपसर्ग प्र, in the सन् formed through यङ् → णिच् → सन् derivation, targeting लिट् परस्मैपद प्रथमपुरुष बहुवचन, starting by applying यङ् to घृ.\n\nFor the ऋकारान्त अङ्ग before यक् (non-kṛt, non-sārvadhātuka), सूत्र \"रीङ् ऋतः\" applies, giving घ्री + य — I'm recalling the parallel with कृ + यङ् → चेक्रीयते to confirm this pattern.\n\nWorking through dvitva for घ्री + यङ्: अभ्यास takes हलादि शेष to give घि, then अभ्यासे चर्च converts घ to ग (jaś substitution of the fourth-class consonant to third), yielding गि as the reduplicated syllable.\n\nApplying कुहोश्चुः converts ग to ज, giving जि, then गुण in यङ्लुक् conditions turns it to जे, producing जेघ्रीयते — which checks out as the standard form for both घ्रा and घृ roots. I'm now considering whether ऋदन्त roots have special provisions in यङ् formation, particularly the rule ऋतश्च (7.4.92) which prescribes रुक्/रिक्/रीक् augments for the अभ्यास when the root ends in ऋ.\n\nAfter confirming that rule applies only to यङ्लुक् and not here, I move to adding णिच् to जेघ्रीय, working through whether अतो लोपः applies and checking the उपधा — since it's ई (गुरु), the पुगन्तलघूपधस्य rule for गुण doesn't trigger, and I'm verifying that अचो ञ्णिति doesn't apply either since the last sound is य्, not a vowel.\n\nNext I'm attaching सन् to जेघ्रीयि, reasoning through why it takes सेट् (इट्) since the base is अनेकाच् and so the अनुदात्त exception doesn't apply, then working through the गुण of णि's इ to ए, the resulting अय् substitution, and the dvitva considerations for सन् given that the dhatu already has an अभ्यास from जे.\n\nChecking this against parallel examples like भावि+सन् → बिभावयिषति and बोभूयि → बोभूययिषति, I conclude जेघ्रीययिष is the correct intermediate form. Now I'm reasoning toward लिट् of this प्रत्ययान्त dhatu, applying \"कास्प्रत्ययादाम् अमन्त्रे लिटि\" to derive जेघ्रीययिषाम् followed by the periphrastic कृ/भू/अस् in परस्मैपद, since सन् after णिच् governs that पद.\n\nAmong the बहुवचन options (चक्रुः, बभूवुः, आसुः), I settle on चक्रुः as the standard choice, giving प्रजेघ्रीययिषाञ्चक्रुः as the final answer.\n\nWorking through the आम् application: सन्-form जेघ्रीययिष loses its final अ before आम् (अतो लोपः), giving जेघ्रीययिषाम्, and the म् becomes anusvāra then ञ् before च — confirming प्रजेघ्रीययिषाञ्चक्रुः. I also double-check that यङ् doesn't interfere here, since यङ् applies to एकाच् हलादि धातुs expressing repeated action, which isn't the case being analyzed.\n\nTurning to रीङ् ऋतः (7.4.27): this substitutes रीङ् for an ऋ-ending root before यक् in non-सार्वधातुक/च्वि contexts, which applies to यङ् formations. For चेक्रीयते the अभ्यास